# Coding standards

The reviewer applies these to every C++ change. `scripts/check` already enforces formatting,
warnings, the clang-tidy rules, sanitizers and test runs, so they are not repeated here.

The reader is {{reader}}. Every rule serves that reader.

## Plain modern C++

Use values, `struct`s, `enum class`, `std::optional`, `std::expected`, `std::variant`,
`std::span`, `std::string_view`, ranges and RAII, plus the approved extras below. Templates beyond
simple function templates, inheritance hierarchies, macros, `new` and `delete`, raw owning
pointers and threads need the owner's approval first. Once approved, they go into the list.

Approved extras:

- None.

Enumerators are CamelCase (`Colour::DarkRed`). ALL_CAPS names look like macros, and the check
rejects the word `DISABLED`.

## Comments

A reader can follow a file from top to bottom by its comments alone.

- Every header starts with a comment that says what the module is for.
- Every declaration in a header has a `//` comment that says what it is for and why.
- Explain a C++ idiom briefly the first time a file uses it, if the reader may not know it. For
  example `std::expected`, a `[[nodiscard]]` attribute or a range adaptor. One short sentence,
  sometimes with the equivalent in a language the reader knows.
- No filler, no praise of the code, no restating the code in words.

## Errors and types

- Expected failures are values: return `std::expected<T, Error>` with the module's error type.
  Exceptions are for the unexpected only, such as a full disk. `main` turns them into exit code 1.
- Give distinct meanings distinct types: `struct UserId { int value; };` instead of a bare `int`,
  so two arguments cannot be swapped.
- Mark a function `[[nodiscard]]` when ignoring its result is a bug. `std::ignore = f();` and
  `(void)f();` throw the result away; the check allows both, so the reviewer asks why.
- No `void*`. A cast to `void*` and back passes every check, and the type is gone.

## Functions and names

- Functions are short, do one thing and read top to bottom. Pull out a named helper instead of
  nesting. Helpers go in an anonymous namespace.
- `const` by default.
- Names say what a value is: `environment`, `argument`. Single letters only in tiny lambdas.
- Use the same name for the same concept everywhere: in code, tests and docs.

## Dependencies

A new `FetchContent` entry or system library needs the owner's approval.

## Tests

- New behaviour comes with a test. A bug fix comes with a test that was red before the fix.
- A pure function with a clear law gets a rapidcheck property.
- Test names describe behaviour.
- Every test asserts something (`EXPECT_*`, `ASSERT_*`, `RC_ASSERT`). The check runs the tests,
  but a test without an assertion passes, and so does a deleted one.
